ratio \code{di} is the disparate impact ratio, with the log interval of Katz
et al. (1978); under the four-fifths rule a ratio below 0.8 against the
group with the highest selection rate is the usual sign of adverse
impact, so by default that group is the base. With the outcomes \code{y},
\code{dtpr} and \code{dfpr} are the gaps in true and false positive rates (both 0
is equalized odds), \code{aod} their mean (the average odds difference) with
the interval of Zou and Donner (2008) built from the two Newcombe
intervals, and \code{eo} the larger of the two gaps in size. The bias
amplification score of Barman and Barman (2026) is large only when a
sizeable gap comes with unequal shares across all the groups; its
